[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is filed as one known issue: the parser reads dates month first,
one fix is queued. Its own cause table already disagrees, and the desk's notes go further. Read
by cause, the reports merged into it are separate problems. Each now has a ticket of its own.

<Callout kind="warn">
The queued parser fix closes only the <code>parser</code> row. The other rows keep coming in after it ships.
</Callout>

## What is in #4471

<ProblemSplit ticket={4471} {issues} list={['one-day']} />

Counts come from the ticket export at render time, so they move as the desk merges more reports.
Change the logic in `src/lib/kb/problems.ts`, not the numbers here.

## The problems

### `parser`: the shared parser reads dates month first

Every tenant on file writes the day first. The shared parser reads every date month first, so a
date whose day is 12 or less comes out with day and month swapped: "Booked for 2 December. The
invite says 12 February." It reaches every tenant, through the app, email and the booking-v2 form.
This is the problem #4471 was named for, and the one the queued fix is for.

### `hint`: booking-v3 tells day-first tenants to type month first

booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People do what the hint says and type the month
first; the form then reads the date the tenant's way, day first. Only reports that came in through
the form of a tenant on booking-v3 have this cause. The swap looks the same as `parser`, but the
parser fix does not touch it: the form has to print the order it reads.

### `one-day`: the date moves by one day

These reports are not swaps. The invite lands a day early or late, or people in different places get
different days for the same meeting: "Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in Toronto got Monday."
That points at time-zone conversion, not date order. The desk read them this way
(`desk-notes.json`), but the kb's cause table still counts them under `parser` because of their
tenant. The parser fix will not move them back.

## Why one ticket held them all

- [Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges reports whose subjects match. Subjects
  describe the symptom ("Wrong day on the invite"), and every problem here has the same symptom.
  The same subject lines turn up under different problems.
- Intake's tag is a guess from the reporter's browser. The "intake tagged as" column shows it
  disagrees with the problem in every row; the tag is not a cause.
- The export repeats some reports across overlapping pages. Counting rows instead of ids
  overstates the ticket.

## What the desk does now

- Keep #4471 for `parser`. Track `hint` and `one-day` in their own tickets, linked in the table.
- Do not close the tickets when the parser fix ships; re-check this page against new reports first.
- Merging on subject alone put different causes in one ticket. Changing that rule needs a new
  decision record that replaces 0001; this page does not change 0001.

